  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving the Business Environment for Social Enterprises to foster social inclusion of disadvantaged groups in Cameroon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Cameroon, strongeconomic growth has not translated into poverty reduction, as population growth outpaces poverty reduction and the number of poor increased by 12 per cent to 8.1 million people between 2007 and 2014. Poverty is also marked by high spatial and social inequities. Poverty rates have only marginally decreased between 2001 and 2014, from 40.2 to 37.5 per cent, with striking and increasing regional disparities: the rural regions of the Far North and the North have poverty levels of 7 per cent and are home to 55.8 per cent of the poor, whereas in urban areas poverty is at 4.8 per cent (3.2 per cent of the poor).  The aim of poverty reduction efforts must be to reduce these spatial and social inequalities of which poverty is an outcome. Improving the terms of participation in society, particularly for people who are disadvantaged, requires enhancing opportunities, access to resources, voice and respect for rights. To drive social inclusion, it is necessary both to address the causes of exclusion and to actively work towards inclusion.  The social and solidarity economy (SSE) has emerged as local collective action to address raising inequalities and social inclusion of certain population groups emerging from market and governance failures to deal with these issues.  Contextually in Cameroon, the Socia</narrative>
  </description>
